WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.510
Hamlet is a play written in about sixteen hundred by
William Shakespeare, and is one of the most

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:10.610
popular and influential stories ever.
Hamlet is set in Denmark, at a time when Norway

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:16.230
is about to attack, and some dodgy stuff is
happening in the royal castle of Elsinore.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.420
Prince Hamlet is the son of King Hamlet and
Queen Gertrude. At the start of the play,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.070
King Hamlet has died, and the king’s brother
Claudius has married Gertrude and taken over

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:30.390
as king. Prince Hamlet is very upset by all
this, moping around, saying he wants to die

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.280
and that everything sucks. He’s especially
angry that Gertrude married Claudius so soon

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.080
after the King’s death.
So Claudius and Gertrude are concerned by

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.330
Hamlet’s behaviour. They try to cheer Hamlet
up, and when that doesn’t work they get

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:46.760
a couple of his old friends, Rosencrantz
and Guildenstern, to spy on him, though Hamlet

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.070
sees through the deception.
Then a ghost appears to Prince Hamlet, claiming

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:55.620
to be his dead father. King Hamlet’s ghost
says that he was murdered by Claudius with

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.900
poison, and demands that Prince Hamlet avenge
him by killing Claudius. Hamlet isn’t entirely

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.440
sure that the ghost really is his dead father,
but he agrees to kill Claudius. He tells his

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.130
friends Horatio and Marcellus that he will
act as though he’s mad, to prevent drawing

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:13.000
suspicion.
Hamlet meets with Ophelia, who he has a romantic

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.050
relationship with. Hamlet acts crazy and awful
to her, telling her he never loved her and

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:23.409
to get to a nunnery. Ophelia’s father, Polonius,
says he thinks Hamlet is mad with love, though

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:26.620
earlier he was saying that Hamlet doesn’t
love her at all.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.030
So Hamlet has a plan to work out for sure
whether Claudius killed King Hamlet. He gets

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.890
a group of actors to perform a play in which
a king is murdered with poison. Claudius leaves

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.740
the room in the middle of the play, which
Hamlet sees as proof that his uncle killed

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:45.090
his father. Later, Hamlet comes across Claudius,
wrestling with his guilt in prayer. Hamlet

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.840
almost kills Claudius there and then, but
he decides not to, saying that Claudius might

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.420
go to heaven if he’s killed while praying.
So then Hamlet is talking with his mother

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.030
Gertrude, when he hears a noise behind a tapestry
in the room. He thinks it’s Claudius, so

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:03.049
he stabs the tapestry but out falls Polonius,
who was spying on the conversation. Having

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.310
killed Polonius, Hamlet berates his mother
a bit more, telling her not to have sex with

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:11.099
Claudius, until the ghost of King Hamlet pops
up again and tells his son to stop mucking

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:15.900
around and kill Claudius already.
Claudius is not feeling terribly safe at this

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.599
point, so he sends Hamlet with Rosencrantz
and Guildenstern to England, with a letter

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:24.359
to the King of England saying to kill Hamlet.
Hamlet finds the letter and changes it so

00:02:24.359 --> 00:02:29.590
it says to kill Rosencrantz and Guildenstern
instead. Meanwhile, Ophelia has gone mad from

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:34.029
the death of her dad and her rejection by
Hamlet, and her brother Laertes, Polonius’

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:39.409
other child, has returned from France furious.
He and Claudius make a plan of their own – they’ll

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:44.450
challenge Hamlet to a fencing match, but Laertes
will have a poisoned sword, and Claudius will

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.689
offer Hamlet poisoned wine.
So Hamlet comes home to Denmark, talks with

00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:53.409
a gravedigger, contemplates death, and finds
out that Ophelia has drowned, apparently in

00:02:53.409 --> 00:03:00.010
a suicidal fit of madness. He has a fistfight
with Laertes over who loved Ophelia more.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.700
And then the fencing match begins. Laertes
cuts Hamlet with his poisoned sword, then

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.590
they accidentally switch swords and Hamlet
cuts Laertes with the poisoned sword. Gertrude

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:15.639
drinks the poisoned wine (seemingly unknowingly),
and Hamlet finally kills Claudius. So almost

00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:20.659
everyone dies, leaving only Horatio to tell the
tale to Fortinbras, a Norwegian prince who

00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:23.799
turns up at the end to take over the Danish
throne.

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:27.859
So that’s Hamlet. There are lots of interesting
questions to be asked about why the characters

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:32.749
do what they do. Like, what’s motivating
Hamlet? What exactly is he so upset about,

00:03:32.749 --> 00:03:37.439
and why does it take him so long to do anything?
Is Hamlet really pretending to be mad, or

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:41.409
does he actually go a bit crazy? What about
his relationship with Ophelia? Did Hamlet

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:44.579
ever love her? And what’s going on with
Gertrude – how much does she

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:49.499
know? There’s lots of stuff going on in
Hamlet about justice and identity and action

00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:53.909
and uncertainty and power and gender, and
that’s part of why Hamlet is so popular

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:58.569
four hundred years after it was written. It’s
also full of really cool lines, really poetic

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:03.480
stuff, and funny stuff. You can easily read
it online if you wanna check it out.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.780
Anyway thanks for watching. Let me know if
you’d like to see more summaries like this.

